From 6241abd8421ea0e2d6b14c91340e51321cf976bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 17:40:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] 3D of a 2160p mode on a display with 3D up to
 1080p: at 1080p, told, by default; 2160p as a choice

1.2.2 kept side by side and top and bottom of a 2160p mode at 2160p on
every display, so that each eye keeps 1920x2160 (a 4K film of two
views, MV-HEVC, has 3840x2160 an eye, and 1080p side by side halves
it again both ways). On a display that declares 3D only up to 1080p,
which is what most 3D TVs declare, nothing then tells the display of
the layout: an RTX 5050 with a 65" TV played a film of 48 frames a
second side by side at 3840x2160 60 Hz, and the TV did not switch to
3D ("Now it does it in 4K so 3D is not triggered").

"Switch the display to 3D (HDMI)" now decides it, display by display,
from what the display declares:
- where it declares the layout at the size of Kodi's mode (2160p), it
  goes out there, told, as in 1.2.2, at both Automatic values;
- where it declares it only at fewer lines at that rate, Automatic (the
  default) takes the largest such timing, 1080p, told by its 3D mode or
  the HDMI vendor InfoFrame, so that the display switches to 3D by
  itself, each eye at 960x1080 or 1920x540, as before 1.2.2;
- "Automatic, keep 2160p", a new value: the layout keeps the lines of
  Kodi's mode, each eye at 1920x2160 or 3840x1080, untold, and the
  display is switched to 3D by hand.
Never fewer lines than a 3D film has (1080) unless Kodi's mode has
fewer itself, as for frame packing, which is not changed.

kodi.log says which: "side-by-side (half) at 1920x1080 @ 60 Hz for
3840x2160 @ 60 Hz: the display takes this 3D layout only up to 1080
lines at this rate ... each eye has 960x1080 (at 3840x2160 @ 60 Hz,
1920x2160, the display switched to 3D by hand: ...)", or, with the new
value, why 2160p is not told. The help of the setting says the same,
and the value is translated (Estuary 54952).
