diff --git a/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po b/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
index d140a69..25f3c86 100644
--- a/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
+++ b/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
@@ -26829,7 +26829,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hades3doutput"; translated in Estuary (#51712)
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#59978"
-msgid "For a 3D TV or projector. While Hades draws in a split 3D mode (over/under or side by side), the display is told so over HDMI and switches to 3D by itself. Frame packing, which keeps both eyes at full resolution, is used where the display and the graphics driver have it (Hades then draws over/under, also for a side-by-side video); otherwise the half format that is drawn. For a film in frame packing, turn on 'Adjust display refresh rate', because HDMI has frame packing for 1080p at 24 Hz only. Where the display has no 3D mode at the refresh rate of a video (a 30p one, say), the 3D mode at twice that rate is used and each picture is shown twice. The display has to be on when Hades starts. Expect one more change of display mode when a 3D video starts and when it stops. Off: the two halves go out as an ordinary 2D picture and you switch the display to 3D by hand. Turn it off if a 3D video shows a black or broken picture. Without a 3D display nothing changes. Applies the next time the display mode is set."
+msgid "For a 3D TV or projector. While Hades draws in a split 3D mode (over/under or side by side), the display is told so over HDMI and switches to 3D by itself. Frame packing, which keeps both eyes at full resolution, is used where the display and the graphics driver have it (Hades then draws over/under, also for a side-by-side video); otherwise the half format that is drawn. For a film in frame packing, turn on 'Adjust display refresh rate', because HDMI has frame packing for 1080p at 24 Hz only. Where the display has no 3D mode at the refresh rate of a video (a 30p one, say), the 3D mode at twice that rate is used and each picture is shown twice. A display that declares 3D only up to 1080p, as most 3D TVs do, gets side by side and top and bottom of a 2160p mode at 1080p, so that it switches to 3D by itself: each eye then has 960x1080 or 1920x540. Automatic, keep 2160p: they stay at 2160p there, each eye at 1920x2160 or 3840x1080, and you switch the display to 3D by hand. Where the display declares them at 2160p, both send 2160p and tell it. The display has to be on when Hades starts. Expect one more change of display mode when a 3D video starts and when it stops. Off: the two halves go out as an ordinary 2D picture and you switch the display to 3D by hand. Turn it off if a 3D video shows a black or broken picture. Without a 3D display nothing changes. Applies the next time the display mode is set."
 msgstr ""
 
 #. Hades: label of setting "videoscreen.hades3doutput"; translated in Estuary (#51711)
@@ -29022,3 +29022,9 @@ msgstr ""
 msgctxt "#62872"
 msgid "Whether this display shows HDR in 3D has not been checked. To check it: LibreELEC settings > System > Check this setup > 3D: HDR."
 msgstr ""
